//
// Phase C1: the negative control for the operator gate.
//
// Runs opcheck.py with a fault injected into one element of one DEVICE input,
// after the FP32 reference has been computed from the clean one, and requires
// the check to FAIL. opcheck.py's --expect-failure reports the control's own
// verdict, so this test passes exactly when the comparison ran and rejected the
// perturbed run -- not merely when the process exited non-zero, which a missing
// toolchain or an absent NPU would also do without comparing anything.
//
// This is the layer a laxer test cannot satisfy, and it is why it is in the
// suite rather than only in the driver. A reference compared against itself, a
// tolerance wide enough to swallow anything, and an ignored --fault-inject flag
// all still report PASS under injection -- and all three fail here. The
// harness's own lesson from Phase A, in
// docs/plans/transformer-layer-execution-studies/14-the-port-loop-harness.md:
// a fix is only trusted after confirming it fails when it should.
//
// One operator, because the injection machinery in opcheck.py is shared by all
// of them and the driver runs the same injection against each of them. Both of
// that operator's shapes run, since omitting a shape key selects them all, and
// both are checked below -- a test that dispatched an injection it did not read
// the verdict of would be spending hardware time for nothing.
//
// NPU serialization lives one layer down, not in the recipe below. Device
// access is locked on /tmp/npu.lock, which XRTRunner.run_test holds across
// xclbin load and dispatch (python/air/backend/xrt_runner.py:292) -- the same
// inode lit.cfg.py's NPU substitution and KernelCache take, so this interlocks
// with every other NPU job in the repository while the peano compiles still
// overlap. Taking the outer /tmp/mlir-air-npu.lock here instead would DEADLOCK:
// the gate command that invokes this suite already holds that inode, and BSD
// flock(2) treats a second open() of the same file as a foreign lock, so the
// nested acquire would block against its own parent until the timeout expires.
//
// Editing this header: lit scans the WHOLE file for its directives, so naming
// one in prose -- even inside a comment -- registers as a second directive and
// leaves the test UNRESOLVED. Describe them, never spell them out.
//
// REQUIRES: ryzen_ai_npu2, peano
//
// RUN: mkdir -p test_npu2_fault_control_peano
// RUN: cd test_npu2_fault_control_peano
// RUN: make -f %S/Makefile clean
// RUN: make -f %S/Makefile check-fault-control PEANO_INSTALL_DIR=%PEANO_INSTALL_DIR | FileCheck %s
//
// The injection line first: it names the element and both values, so an
// injection that silently perturbed nothing is visible here rather than being
// inferred from the verdict.
// CHECK: [fault-inject] input 0 at (511, 0)
// CHECK: [opcheck] elementwise_add [512x512]: FAIL
// CHECK: [fault-inject] input 0 at (4095, 0)
// CHECK: [opcheck] elementwise_add [4096x768]: FAIL
// CHECK: NEGATIVE CONTROL: PASS
